        "content": "{\n  \"quantitative_comparisons\": [\n    {\n      \"group_definitions\": {\n        \"CJRB-101 group\": \"Patients receiving CJRB-101 plus pembrolizumab\",\n        \"NCB group\": \"Patients receiving non-CJRB-101 control (implied comparator in text, though specific control arm details are limited in this excerpt)\"\n      },\n      \"groups\": [\n        {\n          \"group\": \"CJRB-101 group\",\n          \"value\": \"58%\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"result_or_measure\": \"Objective Response Rate (ORR)\",\n      \"setting_or_time\": \"Treatment-naïve NSCLC patients with PD-L1 >50%\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"group_definitions\": {},\n      \"groups\": [\n        {\n          \"group\": \"ICI-refractory NSCLC\",\n          \"value\": \"5%\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"group\": \"ICI-refractory HNSCC\",\n          \"value\": \"0%\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"group\": \"ICI-refractory Melanoma\",\n          \"value\": \"0%\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"result_or_measure\": \"Objective Response Rate (ORR)\",\n      \"setting_or_time\": \"ICI-refractory cohorts\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"usable_content\": \"The 2026 Phase I trial of CJRB-101 (*Leuconostoc mesenteroides*) combined with pembrolizumab provides prospective human evidence linking a specific bacterial strain to immune modulation in solid tumor patients, addressing the gap beyond simple correlation. In treatment-naïve NSCLC patients with PD-L1 expression >50%, the combination yielded an ORR of 58%, numerically higher than historical controls for pembrolizumab monotherapy (44.8%). Mechanistically, the study reports on-treatment upregulation of immune activation genes (CCL5, TLR9, CD3E) in blood/tumor and reduced PD-1+TIM-3+ T cells in PBMCs. Crucially, multiplex immunohistochemistry in a responding patient showed enhanced GZB+CD8+ T-cell infiltration, supporting the preclinical finding that CJRB-101 induces M2-to-M1 macrophage repolarization via TLR4 signaling and enhances CD8+ cytotoxic T-cell activation. Stool metagenomics confirmed strain-specific engraftment. However, the human validation is primarily pharmacodynamic and associational; it lacks a placebo-controlled arm to isolate the strain's effect from the ICI backbone and does not provide direct mechanistic readouts (e.g., TLR4 activation status) in patient tissues. Furthermore, the benefit was marginal or absent in ICI-refractory cohorts (NSCLC ORR 5%; HNSCC/Melanoma 0%), indicating the mechanism's efficacy may depend heavily on patient selection (naïve vs. refractory). Other recent reviews (2025) highlight mechanisms like *Akkermansia*-mediated DC maturation via IL-12, but these are largely synthesized from prior work or hematological contexts, lacking new prospective human validation in solid tumors for 2024-2026.\",\n  \"decision\": \"partial\",\n  \"still_missing\": \"Direct causal validation of the specific strain-mechanism link (e.g., TLR4 pathway activation by CJRB-101) within patient tissues using controlled designs or direct mechanistic biopsies. Confirmation of whether similar strain-specific mechanisms exist for other bacteria (like *Akkermansia* for DC maturation) in prospective human solid tumor trials published between 2024-2026.\",\n  \"read_focus\": \"Check if any other 2024-2026 studies explicitly test *Akkermansia muciniphila* or other specific strains with measured DC cytokine profiles or T-cell clonality in solid tumor patients, as the current material only covers CJRB-101 and general reviews.\",\n  \"external_ask\": \"Search for prospective human studies (2024-2026) in solid tumors that validate strain-specific immune mechanisms (e.g., DC maturation by *Akkermansia*, or other specific strains) with measured immune parameters (DC cytokines, T-cell clonality) beyond clinical correlation.\",\n  \"reason\": \"The CJRB-101 study provides strong prospective human data linking a specific strain to immune changes (macrophage/T-cell modulation) and clinical response